course: dictionary
lesson: 5267
title: Joust: one word, two jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words belong to history, and some are used in everyday talk too. Joust is one of them. Read the context first: «At tournaments knights jousted, charging at each other on horseback with blunted lances in front of cheering crowds.» Knights, horses, lances and cheering crowds tell you that joust is a fight on horseback from long ago.
explain: Now see how one word can stretch to new places. In a debate, politicians «joust» with each other, so the word can mean to argue or compete with words instead of lances. The clues are the people and the talking. Ask yourself: what would fit here? A fight with lances would not make sense in a debate.
explain: Joust can also be a noun. A joust is one contest between two knights, and jousts are many contests. Small endings help too: jousted tells you it happened in the past, and jousting tells you it is happening now. Phrases like «joust with» and «verbal jousting» show you the word in a new text.
fact: To joust means to fight on horseback with long wooden poles called lances.
fact: A joust is one contest between two knights.
fact: To joust can also mean to argue or compete, as people do in a debate.
fact: «Jousting tournament» and «verbal jousting» are useful phrases to remember.
fact: Henry VIII had a bad jousting accident in 1536, and after that and years of a painful leg wound, he grew very heavy later.
exercise: «At tournaments knights jousted, charging at each other on horseback.» What does jousted mean here? | *Fought on horseback with long poles | Ate a big meal together | Sang songs to the crowd | Built a wooden castle
exercise: Politicians «joust» in a debate. What does joust mean here? | Ride very fast in a race | *Argue or compete with words | Cheer loudly for their team | Fight with swords on foot
exercise: Which word means the long wooden poles that knights used in a joust? | *lance | crowd | knight | horse
test: In history lessons, what did knights do at a joust? | Sail ships across the sea | Paint the castle walls | *Charge at each other on horseback with lances | Dig a moat around the castle
test: Which sentence uses joust to mean argue? | *The two candidates jousted with hard questions on television | The lance was made of wood and painted red | The knights jousted on their horses at dawn | The crowds cheered for the joust
exam: What is the difference between a joust and to joust? | *A joust is one contest, and to joust is the action of fighting or arguing | They mean exactly the same thing | To joust is a castle, and a joust is a horse | A joust only happens in cartoons
